    "answer": {
      "short": "$k=0$; $f(x)=1$ for $x\\ne0$.",
      "worked": "The original domain is $x\\ne-k$. For an even function, $f(-x)=f(x)$. For $x\\ne\\pm k$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{-x-k}{-x+k}&=\\frac{x-k}{x+k}\\\\\n\\frac{x+k}{x-k}&=\\frac{x-k}{x+k}\\\\\n(x+k)^2&=(x-k)^2\\\\\nx^2+2kx+k^2&=x^2-2kx+k^2\\\\\n4kx&=0.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThis identity must hold for every $x\\ne\\pm k$, so $k=0$.\n\nWhen $k=0$, the original function is $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x}=1$ for $x\\ne0$. Its domain is symmetric about zero, and $f(-x)=1=f(x)$. Hence $f$ is even.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
